1
00:00:15,897 --> 00:00:34,385
[SPEAKER_00]: When a person begins to contemplate anicca, or if they realize that everything, they think everything is impermanent, what then is the best investment for that person?

2
00:00:34,445 --> 00:00:35,446
[SPEAKER_00]: What should they do about it?

3
00:00:36,426 --> 00:00:39,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Because a lot of people say, well, everything is impermanent.

4
00:00:41,469 --> 00:00:43,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, nothing matters.

5
00:00:45,856 --> 00:00:49,879
[SPEAKER_00]: I can do whatever I want, because it's all going to end, you know, this life.

6
00:00:49,959 --> 00:00:51,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all going to end anyway, so it doesn't matter.

7
00:00:54,582 --> 00:00:59,966
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the face, yeah, in the face of that anxiety of anicca...

8
00:00:59,986 --> 00:01:00,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

9
00:01:00,727 --> 00:01:02,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if they feel anxiety, okay.

10
00:01:02,808 --> 00:01:08,893
[SPEAKER_01]: But a person who is saying, oh, it doesn't matter what I do then, they won't probably even be feeling that anxiety.

11
00:01:09,691 --> 00:01:13,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're feeling anxiety, dismissal will not free you from anxiety.

12
00:01:13,472 --> 00:01:16,933
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say nothing matters, but you still feel anxiety, which is very unpleasant.

13
00:01:16,973 --> 00:01:18,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, what do I do?

14
00:01:19,334 --> 00:01:21,715
[SPEAKER_01]: I can keep saying nothing matters, but anxiety is still there.

15
00:01:21,735 --> 00:01:22,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do?

16
00:01:24,176 --> 00:01:36,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a person does genuinely has sense of anicca through anxiety and recognition of that non-control and, yeah, things will end and that will affect me, well, yeah, they need to recognize it exactly as something

17
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:40,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that must be addressed.

18
00:01:41,772 --> 00:01:44,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like... Something that must be addressed at the root level.

19
00:01:46,537 --> 00:01:50,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Not something that they can just find the answer to, and then move on with their lives.

20
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:54,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, well, those lives are anicca, and that's what they're understanding.

21
00:01:54,524 --> 00:01:56,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, they have to take it seriously.

22
00:01:56,526 --> 00:01:58,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just try to get rid of the discomfort.

23
00:01:58,188 --> 00:02:04,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, tell me what anicca is, tell me what to do so I get rid of this comfort of anxiety, not so I fully understand what anicca is.

24
00:02:05,652 --> 00:02:14,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can temporarily get rid of this discomfort and anxiety, but as I said many times before, anxiety is a symptom of an existential issue.

25
00:02:14,502 --> 00:02:15,163
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a problem.

26
00:02:16,184 --> 00:02:20,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you remove the problem, these symptoms will keep coming back up, one way or the other.

27
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:26,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things will be reminding you of anicca one way or the other, doesn't matter how much you try to deal with it.

28
00:02:28,456 --> 00:02:32,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first thing you do then is take the Buddhist teaching seriously.

29
00:02:32,198 --> 00:02:33,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Take the gradual training seriously.

30
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:40,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't just try to get rid of the current pain that bothers you, but recognize your liability to pain as already being an issue.

31
00:02:41,004 --> 00:02:48,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if everything is fine now and there is no anxiety now, there could be anxiety now and things could not be fine now.

32
00:02:48,168 --> 00:02:50,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, well, that would affect me.

33
00:02:50,310 --> 00:02:54,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, recognition of that is already something that affects you.

34
00:02:54,973 --> 00:02:56,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize this is the level I must practice.

35
00:02:58,285 --> 00:03:00,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take the gradual training seriously then.

36
00:03:00,947 --> 00:03:11,737
[SPEAKER_01]: The precepts, the guarding of the sense doors, and, yeah, reading the suttas, studying and getting the more understanding of what the Buddha was teaching.

37
00:03:13,168 --> 00:03:16,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's dealing with anxiety, dealing with recognition of impermanence.

38
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:29,605
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, well, it's a lifelong task, in a sense of, if you are very committed, you might do it quickly, but your life will then change forever, as somebody who has understood anicca and is free from suffering.

39
00:03:31,363 --> 00:03:37,148
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not doing it quickly, if you're not so committed, it will take you probably a full lifetime to get closer to it.

40
00:03:37,789 --> 00:03:42,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, if you recognize you have to deal with it, your life cannot remain the same.

41
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:47,317
[SPEAKER_01]: As in life of sensuality, carelessness and everything else, leading up to that point.

42
00:03:51,373 --> 00:03:57,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't think anxiety is something, oh, I have a problem with anxiety, something's wrong with me, let me get rid of that, go back... No, no, no.

43
00:03:57,755 --> 00:04:05,496
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom of an underlying problem that shows you that maybe, you know, this life and existence is not the way you would like to think it is, or that everybody else seems to think it is.

44
00:04:06,837 --> 00:04:09,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were so, why are you anxious?

45
00:04:09,778 --> 00:04:19,480
[SPEAKER_01]: If sensuality is enough to keep your mind happy, contented, peaceful, calm, why are you not always happy, contented, peaceful and calm?

46
00:04:21,971 --> 00:04:23,232
[SPEAKER_00]: So, maybe that's not the answer.

47
00:04:24,253 --> 00:04:30,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe the way you're engaging the world and the views you have with the world... Maybe that's why I'm anxious.

48
00:04:30,938 --> 00:04:32,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe my anxiety is a symptom of that.

49
00:04:34,281 --> 00:04:36,263
[SPEAKER_01]: That things actually are not aligned.

50
00:04:37,904 --> 00:04:39,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, Anicca is exactly that.

51
00:04:39,365 --> 00:04:48,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are unownable, not yours, yet here you are, living your life as you're the master controller of your body, your senses, your ownership, people and so on.

52
00:04:49,093 --> 00:04:50,895
[SPEAKER_01]: A complete blatant contradiction.

53
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:54,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Anxiety, every now and then, reminds you of that.

54
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:57,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do something about it before it's too late.

55
00:04:59,360 --> 00:04:59,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is?

56
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:01,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what I just described.

57
00:05:02,181 --> 00:05:15,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Study the suttas, keep the precepts strictly, gradual training, and, yeah, realize that it's a something that will, one way or the other, it needs to affect your life at the very basis of your existence.

58
00:05:15,483 --> 00:05:21,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Not something you can do in response to this particular state, and then I go back to my values from before.

59
00:05:22,502 --> 00:05:27,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the one common response is trying to appreciate everything.

60
00:05:28,583 --> 00:05:29,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Now that everything...

61
00:05:30,164 --> 00:05:32,244
[SPEAKER_00]: I realize everything's anicca, everything's impermanent.

62
00:05:34,265 --> 00:05:35,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's difficult.

63
00:05:35,585 --> 00:05:36,625
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just appreciate it.

64
00:05:37,065 --> 00:05:38,926
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just delight in everything.

65
00:05:39,466 --> 00:05:39,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

66
00:05:40,166 --> 00:05:40,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Even more.

67
00:05:40,826 --> 00:05:41,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Double down.

68
00:05:41,506 --> 00:05:41,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

69
00:05:41,926 --> 00:05:42,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's anicca.

70
00:05:42,606 --> 00:05:44,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Double down in my acceptance.

71
00:05:44,047 --> 00:05:45,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

72
00:05:45,367 --> 00:05:49,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you then find out that you have a terminal cancer, maybe a week to live,

73
00:05:50,376 --> 00:05:55,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that appreciation keep you calm and unaffected and fearless in face of death, not suffering?

74
00:05:56,673 --> 00:06:07,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to equally appreciate and accept that and enjoy, enjoy your chemo and everything else and the torture you have to go through and, you know, seeing your family sort of grieve over you and while you're still alive?

75
00:06:07,159 --> 00:06:08,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Appreciate every moment.

76
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:09,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Appreciate every moment.

77
00:06:09,261 --> 00:06:09,841
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a blessing.

78
00:06:09,981 --> 00:06:10,461
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's not.

79
00:06:11,282 --> 00:06:11,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

80
00:06:11,582 --> 00:06:15,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Life has its blessings, but not all of it is a blessing.

81
00:06:15,144 --> 00:06:16,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the recognition.

82
00:06:16,585 --> 00:06:17,286
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

83
00:06:17,326 --> 00:06:18,206
[SPEAKER_01]: If the life is not...

84
00:06:18,827 --> 00:06:23,910
[SPEAKER_01]: If the life were only the life of misery, beings would not be enamored with it.

85
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:29,142
[SPEAKER_01]: If the life were only life of blessings and appreciation, well, beings would not be disgusted with it.

86
00:06:30,063 --> 00:06:33,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, life is both, and you use wisdom to recognize.

87
00:06:33,824 --> 00:06:37,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, don't be irrationally averse to everything.

88
00:06:37,406 --> 00:06:49,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there are blessings, there are things you should appreciate, but recognize that it's all within the nature of things not being in control, and the nature of things being subject to change in its core, and that is inaccessible to you.

89
00:06:49,692 --> 00:06:51,453
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't affect that, you can't prevent that.

90
00:06:52,516 --> 00:06:56,818
[SPEAKER_01]: So, keeping that in mind, then, you put your appreciation in perspective.

91
00:06:57,759 --> 00:07:00,100
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't use appreciation to deal with anicca.

92
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:03,522
[SPEAKER_01]: You use anicca to not get careless with your appreciation.

93
00:07:04,983 --> 00:07:09,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, you use anicca to not get careless with your non-appreciation or aversion.

94
00:07:09,585 --> 00:07:14,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you use anicca, wisdom of recognition of the nature of things, to keep yourself in check.

95
00:07:15,889 --> 00:07:20,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in order to do that, you need to stop trying to get rid of it through appreciation or whatever else you might be doing.

96
00:07:20,891 --> 00:07:22,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only reason people would be doing it.

97
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:24,581
[SPEAKER_01]: To get rid of discomfort of it.

98
00:07:26,102 --> 00:07:26,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no, no, no.

99
00:07:26,562 --> 00:07:27,543
[SPEAKER_01]: I accept this, actually.

100
00:07:28,303 --> 00:07:29,044
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not up to you.

101
00:07:29,704 --> 00:07:32,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, the mountains coming to crush you.

102
00:07:32,066 --> 00:07:33,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I accept that.

103
00:07:33,226 --> 00:07:33,386
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

104
00:07:33,987 --> 00:07:35,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Who are you to even accept that?

105
00:07:35,348 --> 00:07:36,228
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say.

106
00:07:36,909 --> 00:07:40,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you accept or not, this is the inevitable outcome.

107
00:07:40,971 --> 00:07:47,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you saying that you appreciate and accept this, it's just you trying to appease yourself in the face of something you have no control over.

108
00:07:50,577 --> 00:07:51,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's unpleasant.

109
00:07:52,365 --> 00:07:57,368
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to stop trying to get rid of it, recognize it as a symptom, and then, gradual training.

110
00:07:57,388 --> 00:07:59,229
[SPEAKER_00]: You're trying to get control again.

111
00:07:59,249 --> 00:08:00,650
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to repossess it, exactly.

112
00:08:00,670 --> 00:08:01,571
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

113
00:08:01,611 --> 00:08:02,972
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted that to happen.

114
00:08:03,012 --> 00:08:03,552
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you didn't.

115
00:08:05,733 --> 00:08:10,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody wants that to happen.

116
00:08:12,338 --> 00:08:12,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah.

117
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:20,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Gradual picking up, gradual training, gradual development, gradual composing,

118
00:08:21,998 --> 00:08:24,000
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say gradual, I don't mean take it easy.

119
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:26,502
[SPEAKER_01]: I just mean you do need to do it gradually.

120
00:08:26,563 --> 00:08:33,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to start on that level of virtue, unblemished, unbroken virtue.

121
00:08:33,229 --> 00:08:35,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take it seriously, the behavior.

122
00:08:35,091 --> 00:08:42,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, from there, you know, your speech will become more wholesome than even your thoughts.

123
00:08:42,298 --> 00:08:44,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you will be able to actually

124
00:08:46,267 --> 00:08:57,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Withstand more of the recognition of anicca and anxiety without having too much pressure of, I must deal with this, and then jump into one view or the other, or one action or the other, because it's unbearable to you.

125
00:08:57,632 --> 00:09:00,994
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason it's unbearable, because the mind is too weak in relation to it.

126
00:09:01,894 --> 00:09:06,536
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is too weak, because you haven't been practicing sense restraining God, in the sense those taming the beasts.

127
00:09:06,596 --> 00:09:09,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't been doing that, so they're still very powerful.

128
00:09:10,762 --> 00:09:18,492
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, how can you then be in control of them, unless you can physically, at least, be able to go around them, and not let them drag you around?

129
00:09:20,118 --> 00:09:25,520
[SPEAKER_01]: That composure, they will be able to face the anxiety or any other unpleasantness, will be there.

130
00:09:26,621 --> 00:09:28,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you build a basis for it.

131
00:09:28,742 --> 00:09:29,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, oh, how do I face anxiety?

132
00:09:30,002 --> 00:09:30,082
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

133
00:09:30,122 --> 00:09:32,243
[SPEAKER_01]: You are already facing it by not acting out of it.

134
00:09:33,024 --> 00:09:36,585
[SPEAKER_01]: By not breaking your virtue when you're anxious, you are already facing anxiety.

135
00:09:36,645 --> 00:09:37,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You are doing it.

136
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:39,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it doesn't feel like that.

137
00:09:39,827 --> 00:09:40,627
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the work.

138
00:09:42,048 --> 00:09:44,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Not when you think you're doing the work or not.

139
00:09:44,749 --> 00:09:44,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

140
00:09:45,009 --> 00:09:45,609
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a mountain.

141
00:09:45,629 --> 00:09:46,230
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a mountain.

142
00:09:47,071 --> 00:09:47,551
[SPEAKER_01]: There you are.

143
00:09:47,952 --> 00:09:50,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And there were suttas where everybody said, what is samādhi?

144
00:09:50,213 --> 00:10:03,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi is basically a monk who practices mind... basically the mind... a monk who practices keeping his mind unmoved by things they want to move it.

145
00:10:03,663 --> 00:10:07,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need to seek out things they want to move it in terms of temptations and so on.

146
00:10:07,606 --> 00:10:10,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Your senses already want to move it all the time.

147
00:10:10,949 --> 00:10:13,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're just alone in a cell without any distraction.

148
00:10:14,908 --> 00:10:15,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's samādhi.

149
00:10:16,328 --> 00:10:20,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So, immovability of the mind begins by immovability of behavior.

150
00:10:22,510 --> 00:10:30,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Unbroken virtue, the aggregate of virtue, you have created, the immovable aggregate of certain non-behavior.

151
00:10:31,153 --> 00:10:35,394
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not behave towards sensuality, towards, you know, actions of ill-will and so on.

152
00:10:37,035 --> 00:10:38,455
[SPEAKER_01]: And it becomes this solid thing.

153
00:10:39,572 --> 00:10:45,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Of certain behavior is not to be done, hasn't been done, you've accomplished that, and that's your aggregate of virtue, as the Buddha referred to it.

154
00:10:48,074 --> 00:10:52,435
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sense that this body is this solid aggregate, well, your virtue becomes this solid aggregate.

155
00:10:52,495 --> 00:10:54,816
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't spill over, you don't break through there.

156
00:10:55,896 --> 00:10:57,857
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the beginning of samādhi.

157
00:10:58,957 --> 00:11:00,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla, samādhi, pañña.

158
00:11:00,157 --> 00:11:02,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Not sīla over there, then you run and find samādhi.

159
00:11:04,438 --> 00:11:06,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla gives the basis for samādhi.

160
00:11:06,479 --> 00:11:07,399
[SPEAKER_01]: On that basis,

161
00:11:08,324 --> 00:11:13,307
[SPEAKER_01]: And that gives the basis for clear seeing, on that basis.

162
00:11:13,347 --> 00:11:23,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Personally, yeah, the aggregates of virtue, you build that up, you create... You build that up, and stop eroding it, stop taking it conditionally, and stop sort of thinking it's kind of a side thing.

163
00:11:24,432 --> 00:11:26,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Your practice comes through the framework of your virtue.

164
00:11:28,675 --> 00:11:29,155
[SPEAKER_00]: No other way.

